# TypeScript Type Aliases

Use `type` instead of `interface` for all type definitions.

## Rules

1. Always use `type` with object syntax:
   ```typescript
   // Good
   type User = {
     id: number;
     name: string;
   }
   
   // Bad
   interface User {
     id: number;
     name: string;
   }
   ```

2. For extending React types, use intersection:
   ```typescript
   // Good
   export type ButtonProps = 
     React.ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement> & {
     variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary';
   }
   
   // Bad
   export interface ButtonProps 
     extends React.ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement> {
     variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary';
   }
   ```

3. For composing multiple types:
   ```typescript
   // Good
   type Combined = BaseProps & VariantProps & {
     extra: string;
   }
   
   // Bad
   interface Combined extends BaseProps, VariantProps {
     extra: string;
   }
   ```

## Why Types Over Interfaces

- More flexible (unions, intersections, mapped types)
- Consistent syntax throughout codebase
- Better for functional programming patterns
- Clearer semantics (aliases vs contracts)
